# b.requires("RANGE") -- the project-side version pin (NEXT_STEPS_to_1.0 item 3,
# 2026-09-15). The compiler compiling this file checks its own `_zbr_version`
# against the literal range in the FRONT END and refuses by name when outside it
# (tools/cli_check.sh has the refusing leg with `^99.0`; test/fail_fixtures/
# build_requires_rejected_test.zbr pins the diagnostic text). This fixture is the
# satisfied side: every form of range the check accepts. The `>=` forms hold for any
# 0.9 or later compiler; the caret, tilde and exact forms are pinned to the CURRENT
# line on purpose and must be BUMPED when `_zbr_version` leaves it -- that is the
# fixture reminding us the check is real. Moved from the 0.9 line to the 1.0 line
# on 2026-11-09, when main became 1.0.0-dev (a prerelease counts as its release, so
# 1.0.0-dev satisfies `1.0.0`; under it, `^0.9` -- major 0, same minor -- refuses).
def main()
    var b = Build.new()
    b.requires(">=0.9")
    b.requires(">=0.9.0")
    b.requires("^1.0")          # same major, at least 1.0.0
    b.requires("~1.0.0")        # same major.minor
    b.requires("1.0.0")         # exact (a prerelease of 1.0.0 counts as 1.0.0)
    b.requires("=1.0.0")
    var app = b.exe("req_app", "test/toplevel_main_test.zbr")
    print("build requires: ok")
